                    CALL FOR PAPERS

Workshop on Quality of Service in Open Distributed Processing
^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^

This workshop will be part of the programme of the 2nd International
Conference on Open Distributed Processing (ICODP'95) to be held in Brisbane,
Australia, 20 - 24 February, 1995.

SCOPE:

Results on Quality of Service (QoS) related research have been achieved
in the specific areas, e.g.  communication protocols, operating systems,
non-continuous media file servers, and coding schemes. The Workshop on Quality
of Service in Open Distributed Processing aims to extend the application
of such results to a system level, in particular in the context of ODP systems.

The workshop organisers encourage contributions which focus on the
integration of QoS in distributed environments on the modelling,
specification, implementation and application level.


AREAS OF INTEREST:

  - QoS description and specification
  - QoS negotiation and adaptation
  - QoS monitoring
  - QoS based interface binding 
  - QoS in distributed environments
  - QoS in standardisation
  - QoS based management
  - QoS dependent configuration and re-configuration

INFORMATION FOR AUTHORS:

Contributions are expected either as "papers" or as "position statements".

Papers

Papers will be refereed.  They should not exceed 12 pages. It is
anticipated that presented papers will be published through IFIP
by Chapman & Hall, London.  Papers should include an abstract and keywords.
The conference language is English. Please submit one copy only of your
paper to the organisers.


Position Statements

The purpose of position statements is to provide a seed for debate and
discussion.  The conference organisers encourage participants to present
suggestions for future directions and concerns about current developments.
Position Statements should not exceed 4 pages.  Please submit one copy
only of your contribution to the organizers.


All papers and position statements can be submitted via electronic
mail (PostScript only), facsimile (submission only for this method)
or postal mail.  The preferred method for submission is electronic mail.
The correspondence addresses for all three methods are given below.

