I missed the original posting -- was it a just call for discussion
about a comp.sys.dce group?  It'd be nice to have a less widely
cross-posted discussion ... and not to confuse CORBA with DCE, as
the two are quite distinct.

Walter Roberson <roberson@hamer.ibd.nrc.ca> wrote:
> I am not clear on the relationship between DCE, COBRA, and IDL (Interface
> Definition Language.)

DCE is one distributed system infrastructure.  Read about it in the
marketing press, or find friends who use it to run their companies (!)
and ask them about it.  Some of the OSF newsgroups surely talk about
it, as does comp.client-server, and you'll find discussions about it
in various other areas too.

CORBA is another, at an earlier stage of development and generally
uninvolved with DCE.  It's the Object Management Group's (OMG's)
base standard for APIs to an Object Request Broker (ORB).  (Some folk
want to build CORBA systems atop DCE, but that's just an implementation
detail that no user of CORBA can rely on.  Like a CPU instruction set.)
I think most discussions about CORBA and ORBs go on in comp.object, and
in comp.client-server even though ORBs aren't just client/server.

IDL is admittedly confusing.  Several different systems use that
acronym.  I find it useful to talk about OMG-IDL (the Interface
Definition Language used by OMG, which is part of CORBA), MS-IDL (the
one used by Microsoft), DCE-IDL (the one used by OSF/DCE, the core of
MS-IDL), and otherwise prefix the term with some adjective.  (I suspect
the "Interactive Display Language" predate most of those ... what's a
good prefix for that one?)

